AM–GM does not give a pointwise constant comparison between \(Z\) and its upper bound: \(Z\) can vanish while \(Q(P)+Q(P')\) is positive. That causes no failure here. The averaged upper bound for the squares has exactly the required scale.

Similarly, enlargement is not a pointwise relative approximation. Under the filter,
\[
tT\asymp_d MHV^d\asymp_d X^*,
\]
so its geometric enlargement has bounded scale. BDH_fam controls the resulting mass, including values near zero, without an \(L^{\Theta(1)}\) loss.

For Step 5, the direction and sign are right:
\[
g=(g_0^*,g_1^*)+j(a,b),\qquad
G_g(P')=G_{g^*}(P')-j\Delta.
\]
Thus the signed progression has difference \(-\Delta/\ell=\pm q\).

After reflection, the filtered intervals lie in \([X_r/64,X_r]\). For sufficiently large \(N\), \(R_0\ge64\), so they can be enlarged inside \([X_r/R_0,X_r]\). Moreover,
