   275	Consequently, with the envelope V₋,
   276	  E_ℓ Σ_{n∈𝒩_ℓ} 𝔔(ℓ²|n|q)/φ(ℓ|n|) ≤ τ(q)(log N)^{O(1)}·Ψ(X′₊)Ψ(X*)²/(Y q (MV₋)²).   (4.1)
   277	
   278	*Proof.* Recall ℓ > max(B, 2S²) ≥ max(P⁺(n), q), so ℓ ∤ |n|q.
   279	- **Rearrangement.** f(m′) does not depend on ℓ, because X* is common to all ℓ ∈ J. So the left side of
   280	  (***) is Σ_{m′>1} f(m′)ν(m′), with ν(m′) := E_ℓ #{n ∈ 𝒩_ℓ : m′ | ℓ²|n|q}.
   281	- **Partition (independent of ℓ).**
   282	  - (A): m′ has no prime factor in J, and 1 < m′ ≤ R₀.
   283	  - (B): m′ has no prime factor in J, and m′ > R₀.
   284	  - (C): m′ has a prime factor in J.
   285	  - Since R₀ < Y, the classes are disjoint and exhaustive.
   286	  - For (A) and (B), m′ | ℓ²|n|q ⟺ m′ | |n|q, for every ℓ.
   287	  - For (C), m′ | ℓ²|n|q forces ℓ | m′. Then ℓ is m′'s unique J-prime factor, with exponent i ∈ {1, 2}. An m′
   288	    with two J-prime factors has ν = 0.
   289	- **(A).**
   290	  - *Bound on f.* f(m′) ≤ Σ_{a mod m′}|S(a/m′)|² = m′Σ_c N_c², with N_c := #{F ∈ 𝒮* : F ≡ c (mod m′)}. DS on
   291	    ±[X*R₀^{−1}, X*], plus the trivial bound X*R₀^{−1}/m′ + 1 on |F| < X*R₀^{−1}, gives
   292	    N_c ≤ (X*/m′)ϱ(X*)(log N)^{O(1)}. This uses R₀^{−1} ≤ ϱ(X*) and m′ ≤ X*ϱ(X*). Hence
   293	    f(m′) ≤ m′·max_c N_c·#𝒮* ≤ Ψ(X*)²(log N)^{O(1)}.
   294	  - *Bound on ν.* Let m₂ := gcd(m′, q) and m₁ := m′/m₂. Then m′ | |n|q ⟺ m₁ | n, since gcd(m₁, q/m₂) = 1.
   295	    If m₁ is not B-smooth, ν = 0. Otherwise, by (Hi) and (C8.16),
   296	    ν ≤ 2Ψ(X′₊/m₁) ≤ 4(X′₊/m₁)ϱ(X′₊R₀^{−1}) ≤ (Ψ(X′₊)/m₁)(log N)^{O(C₀)}.
   297	  - The map m′ ↦ (m₁, m₂) is injective, with m₂ | q. So Σ_{m′≤R₀} 1/m₁ ≤ τ(q)(1 + log R₀).
   298	  - Hence (A) ≤ τ(q)(log N)^{O(1)}Ψ(X′₊)Ψ(X*)².
   299	- **(B).**
   300	  - Here m′ ≤ X′₊q. On dyadic blocks (R, 2R], R = R₀2^i: ν(m′) ≤ #{1 ≤ |n| ≤ X′₊ : m₁ | n} ≤ 2X′₊/m₁ ≤ 2X′₊q/R.
   301	  - By (LS) with K = 2⌊X*⌋ + 1 and Q = 2R: Σ_{m′∈(R,2R]} f(m′) ≤ (3X* + 4R²)·3Ψ(X*).
   302	  - So (B) ≤ 36X′₊qX*Ψ(X*)/R₀ + 192X′₊²q²Ψ(X*).
   303	  - First term: 36X′₊qX*Ψ(X*)/R₀ ≤ Ψ(X′₊)Ψ(X*)², because R₀ ≥ 36q/(ϱ(X′₊)ϱ(X*)).
   304	  - Second term: 192X′₊q² ≤ ϱ(X′₊)ϱ(X*)X*, because X*/X′₊ ≥ c_dHV₋^{d−1}Y ≥ N^h and q²/(ϱϱ) = L^{O(1)}.
   305	- **(C).**
   306	  - Only ℓ = ℓ(m′) contributes, so ν(m′) = π_J^{−1}#{n ∈ 𝒩_ℓ : m″ | |n|q}, where m″ := m′/ℓ^i.
   307	  - On a block (R, 2R] with Y/2 ≤ R ≤ 4Y²X′₊q (O(log N) blocks), two bounds hold: ν ≤ 3Ψ(X′₊)/π_J, and, since
   308	    m″ > R/(4Y²), ν ≤ 8X′₊qY²/(π_JR).
   309	  - Using the first bound against 3X* and the second against 4R²:
   310	      (C) ≤ π_J^{−1}[27 log N·Ψ(X′₊)X*Ψ(X*) + 192X′₊qY²·8Y²X′₊q·Ψ(X*)].
   311	  - First term: X*/π_J ≤ Ψ(X*)/log² N, since π_J = N^{2ν+o(1)} and 1/ϱ(X*) = L^{O(1)}.
   312	  - Second term: it suffices that X′₊q²Y³ log Y ≤ c·ϱϱ·X*. Since X*/X′₊ ≥ c_dHV₋^{d−1}Y, this follows from
   313	    h + (d−1)ν > 4ν, i.e. h > (5−d)ν. The Kern floor h ≥ 2ν + η₀ implies this with margin, for all d ≥ 3.
   314	- **(4.1).**
   315	  - For n ∈ 𝒩_ℓ: m := ℓ²|n|q ≥ ℓqMV₋/32 and φ(ℓ|n|) ≥ cMV₋/log log N.
   316	  - So 𝔔(m)/φ(ℓ|n|) ≤ C log log N·[(#𝒮*)² + Σ_{m′|m, m′>1} f(m′)]/(Yq(MV₋)²).
   317	  - Sum over n, average over ℓ, and use #𝒩_ℓ ≤ 3Ψ(X′₊) and (***). ∎
   318	
   319	(Exponent audit by codex ldbound_2, ratios to the target Ψ(X′₊)Ψ(X*)²:
   320	- (B) first term: q/(R₀ρ_rρ_F);
   321	- (B) second term: N^{−h−(d+1)ν}L^{O(1)};
   322	- (C) first term: N^{−2ν}L^{O(1)};
   323	- (C) second term: N^{(5−d)ν−h}L^{O(1)}.
   324	All are small. `params.margins` checks the exponents.)
   325	
   326	*Remarks.*
   327	- **What is used.**
   328	  - No Siegel–Walfisz input and no equidistribution are used.
   329	  - The pointwise DS bound covers conductors ≤ B^{C₀} for any fixed C₀.
   330	  - The large sieve's diagonal X*Ψ(X*) is beaten in two ways: by the divisibility sparsity
   331	    ν(m′) ≲ X′₊q/m′ in class (B), and by the average over N^{2ν} primes ℓ in class (C).
   332	- **The ℓ-average is essential in (C).**
   333	  - For one fixed ℓ, the frequencies with denominator ℓ² contribute ℓ²Σ_c N_c² over classes mod ℓ². Its
   334	    trivial bound is X*Ψ(X*) ≫ Ψ(X*)².
   335	  - A single-modulus bound would need smooth numbers to be L²-equidistributed mod ℓ² = B^{Θ(u)}.
   336	  - That is unconditionally out of reach: the primes ≤ B could lie in a subgroup of (Z/ℓ²)^× of index up
   337	    to 1/ϱ(ℓ², B) = L^{Θ(1)}, since Burgess-type bounds are far above B = ℓ^{o(1)} (cf. Soundararajan,
   338	    CRM Proc. 46 (2008); Harper 2012).
   339	- **Codex's blind proof is different.** Its Theorem AR (`notes/ldbound_codex1_report.md` §3) proves the
   340	  same statement through multiplicative characters: induced-character multiplicities, Harper's Prop. 1
   341	  and zero-density results for the ℓ, ℓ² conductors, and exact gcd layers for non-reduced classes. Its
   342	  worst exponent is the same, h + (d−5)ν > 0. The additive route above avoids the induced-character
   343	  bookkeeping.
   344	- **Prop B5 is not contradicted.** L2_fam is an L² statement over all classes. It never passes through
   345	  max-class ratios.
   346	- **Attribution (literature critic).**
   347	  - The route is the classical large-sieve route to Barban–Davenport–Halberstam upper bounds (Gallagher,
   348	    Mathematika 14 (1967); Montgomery, Michigan Math. J. 17 (1970)).
   349	  - The rearrangement Σ f(m′)·#{moduli divisible by m′} is in the spirit of Hooley's switching
   350	    (J. reine angew. Math. 274/275 (1975)) and of Harper 2025's "non-concentration on multiples".
   351	  - Smooth-number BDH-type bounds over *all* moduli: Harper, arXiv 1208.5992, Thm 2 (preprint), with
   352	    saving H(u)^{−c}. It does not imply L2_fam: the family's main term is Ψ²·L^{−Θ(1)}, below Harper's
   353	    error term Ψ²L^{−o(1)}.
   354	  - Upper bounds of this type over all moduli are folklore: Granville, MSRI Publ. 44 (2008) §4.2,
   355	    attributes one to Granville–Vaughan.
   356	  - Harper, J. London Math. Soc. 112 (2025) e70293, Cor. 1 requires x^{0.51} ≤ Q ≤ x and does not apply.
   357	  - We found no statement of L2_fam itself. What is specific here is the restricted family, the L^{o(1)}
   358	    bookkeeping and the ℓ-average.
   359	
   360	## 5. Theorem LD* for (a‴_k; μ)
   361	
   362	**Theorem LD\* [proved].**
   363	- **Hypotheses.** §1 and §2, with S² = (log N)^{C₃}/(ρ_rρ_F) as in pcest Theorem 4.3, and N_ℓ(A) the filtered
   364	  count under the moment law 𝒜‴.
   365	- **Conclusion.** E[N(N−1)] ≤ (log N)^{O(1)}·S⁴ρ_r²ρ_F².
   366	- The same argument applied to Z (Step 1) also proves the bound in pcest's normalization:
   367	    E_{ℓ,w}Σ_{P≠P′, ℓ|Δ}|𝒢|^{−1}Σ_g Y_PY_{P′}(S²/q)κ_alg ≤ (log N)^{O(1)}S⁴ρ_r².
   368	  This uses (S²/q)κ_alg ≍ Z/(4d²H²V^{2d−4}ϱ(T|G|)ϱ(T|G′|)) and ϱ(T|G|) ≥ ρ_F/(log N)^{O(1)} under the filter.
   369	- With FM: PC ≤ (log N)^{O(1)}, so D_k(γ) holds with γ ≥ L^{−o(1)} (Prop PZ).
   370	
   371	*Proof.* Put T := dHV^{d−1}, t := |G_g(P)|, t′ := |G_g(P′)| and q := |Δ|/ℓ ∈ [1, 2S²]. Pair sums run over ordered
   372	pairs P ≠ P′ in 𝒫_ℓ ∩ Λ_ℓ(g), with ℓ | Δ and both filtered.
   373	- **Step 0 (law).** For ℓ | N, N_ℓ := 0. For ℓ ∤ N, under 𝒜‴,
   374	    E[N(N−1)] = E_{ℓ,g,w} Σ_{P≠P′} Y_PY_{P′}·Pr_c[F_A(P), F_A(P′) ∈ 𝒮_B].
   375	  - Pairs with ℓ ∤ Δ contribute nothing (pcest Lemma Q, step 0).
   376	  - Only F̂_{g,w} depends on w.
   377	- **Step 1 (kernel).**
   378	  - Lemma Kern(iii), with ‖v ∧ v′‖ ≥ |Δ|(bb′)^{d−2} ≥ |Δ|(V/2)^{2d−4}, gives Pr_c[Φ(c) = z] ≤ C_d/(H²V^{2d−4}).
   379	  - Φ(C_H) ⊂ [−T, T]², and Γ = {z₂ ≡ κ^{d−1}z₁ (mod Δ)} (Kern(i)), where P′ ≡ κP (mod Δ). Hence
   380	      Pr_c[both smooth] ≤ (C_d/(H²V^{2d−4}))·Z,  Z := Σ_{j mod |Δ|} A_j(P)A_{κ^{d−1}j}(P′),
   381	    with A_j(P) := #{|z| ≤ T : z ≡ j (mod |Δ|), F̂(P) + G(P)z ∈ 𝒮_B}.
   382	- **Step 2 (AM–GM).**
   383	  - κ is a unit mod Δ, since P′ is primitive.
   384	  - So Z ≤ ½(Q(P) + Q(P′)), with Q(P) := Σ_j A_j(P)², which depends on (P, g, w, |Δ|) only.
   385	  - This is not a pointwise comparison (Z may vanish while Q > 0), but both sides have the same
   386	    main-term scale.
   387	- **Step 3 (enlargement).**
   388	  - z ↦ F = F̂(P) + G(P)z is injective, with F ≡ r₀ (mod t). Also z ≡ z′ (mod |Δ|) ⟺ F ≡ F′ (mod t|Δ|), for
   389	    either sign of G. All such F lie in [−X*, X*] (§1). Hence
   390	      Q(P) ≤ 𝔔₀(t, |Δ|; r₀) := #{(F, F′) ∈ 𝒮*² : F ≡ F′ ≡ r₀ (mod t), F ≡ F′ (mod t|Δ|)}.
   391	  - 𝔔₀ is a fixed function of r₀ that does not depend on w.
   392	  - Under the filter, tT ≍_d X*, so the enlargement has bounded scale.
   393	- **Step 4 (average over w).**
   394	  - Both candidates have Y = 1, so (1.1) and Corollary W apply. The classes c partition the counted
   395	    pairs, so
   396	      E_w 𝔔₀(t, |Δ|; r₀) ≤ (C_W/φ(t)) Σ_{c∈(Z/t)^×} 𝔔₀(t, |Δ|; c) ≤ (C_W/φ(t))·𝔔(t|Δ|).
   397	  - The same holds for P′.
   398	  - The sum is invariant under (P, P′) ↦ (P′, P), with Δ ↦ −Δ and symmetric filter and weights. Hence
   399	      E[N(N−1)] ≤ (C_dC_W/(H²V₋^{2d−4}))·E_{ℓ,g} Σ_{P≠P′} Y_PY_{P′}·𝔔(tℓq)/φ(t).   (5.1)
   400	  - No joint law of (r₀(P), r₀(P′)) under the shared w is needed.
   401	- **Step 5 (fibres; the partner's rational weight).**
   402	  - Fix ℓ, P = (a, b) and P′ with |Δ| = ℓq. The algebraic factor 𝔔(tℓq)/φ(t) depends on g only through
   403	    n := G_g(P)/ℓ, and Y_P = 1 ⟺ n ∈ 𝒩_ℓ.
   404	  - For fixed n, the g with G_g(P) = ℓn are g = g* + j(a, b) in (g₀, g₁)-coordinates. The direction is
   405	    primitive with |(a, b)| > V/2, so there are ≤ √2M/V + 1 values of j.
   406	  - Along the fibre, G_g(P′) = G_{g*}(P′) − jΔ. So n′ = G_g(P′)/ℓ runs through a progression of difference q,
   407	    in an interval of length ≤ A := q(√2M/V + 1).
   408	  - The filter puts |n′| ∈ [X_r/64, X_r]. For each sign, enlarge the n′-interval to length
   409	    max(|I_±|, X_rB^{−C₀}) inside [X_rB^{−C₀}, X_r], which needs R₀ ≥ 64.
   410	  - DS (modulus q ≤ 2S² ≤ R₀; X_rB^{−C₀}/q ≤ M/V) then gives
   411	      #{g ∈ 𝒢 : G_g(P) = ℓn, Y_{P′} = 1, P′ filtered} ≤ U := (3M/V₋)ρ_r(log N)^{O(1)},
   412	    uniformly.
   413	  - Hence Σ_g Y_PY_{P′}𝔔(tℓq)/φ(t) ≤ U·Σ_{n∈𝒩_ℓ} 𝔔(ℓ²|n|q)/φ(ℓ|n|).
   414	- **Step 6 (partners).** For fixed P and q, at most 12 points P′ ∈ 𝒫_ℓ have det(P, P′) = ±ℓq. They lie on two
   415	  lines parallel to P; on each, consecutive integer points differ by P, with |P| ≥ V/2, inside a segment
   416	  of length ≤ 2√2V.
   417	- **Step 7 (assembly).**
   418	  - Bound all ℓ-dependent geometric factors by their envelopes. These are #𝒫_ℓ ≤ CV_max²/log V₋,
   419	    |𝒢| ≥ cM², U and V^{−(2d−4)} ≤ V₋^{−(2d−4)}; V_max/V₋ ≤ 2. Then apply (4.1):
   420	      E[N(N−1)] ≤ C·(H²V₋^{2d−4})^{−1}M^{−2}·V_max²·(M/V₋)ρ_r·Σ_{q≤2S²} τ(q)(log N)^{O(1)}Ψ(X′₊)Ψ(X*)²/(Yq(MV₋)²).
   421	  - Here Ψ(X′₊) ≤ (MV_max/Y)ρ_r(log N)^{O(1)} and Ψ(X*) ≤ C MHV_max^dρ_F(log N)^{O(1)}, by (Hi) and (C8.16).
   422	  - Σ_{q≤2S²} τ(q)/q ≪ (1 + log S)².
   423	  - The powers give V⁴/Y² ≍ S⁴, up to constants from the floors.
   424	  - Hence E[N(N−1)] ≤ (log N)^{O(1)}S⁴ρ_r²ρ_F².
   425	  - **PC.** By FM, (E N)² ≥ S⁴ρ_r²ρ_F²/(log N)^{O(1)} and E N ≥ 1, so PC ≤ (log N)^{O(1)}. ∎
   426	
   427	*Remarks.*
   428	- **No large-values input appears.** The residue is integrated out over w (Step 4) before any arithmetic
   429	  estimate is applied. The partner's rational weight is then handled by divisor switching along fibres
   430	  (Step 5). That is possible only because the algebraic factor no longer depends on the fibre index.
   431	- **Theorem R is not used.** The rational pair correlation is re-derived inside Step 5.
   432	- **Uniformity.** All constants depend only on (d, k, μ, a, the σ-range, η₀). Every "for large N" is a
   433	  polynomial margin (`params.margins`) or an L^{O(1)} comparison.
   434	- **LD versus LD\*.** pcest's literal LD has right side L^{o(1)} × (rational pair count). We prove the
   435	  bound Theorem 4.3 consumes. The two agree if the rational pair count is ≥ S⁴ρ_r²L^{−o(1)}; that lower
   436	  bound is not needed, and we do not prove it.
   437	- **Independent confirmation.** Codex's blind round found the same order of operations:
   438	  - a pointwise AM–GM split κ_alg(z) ≤ ½(K_P(z) + K_{P′}(z)) under one shared multiplier;
   439	  - the multiplier average;
   440	  - fibre divisor switching.
   441	  Its multiplier is z uniform in [1, N^h] with F = zF̂ + GR, rather than a prime w. Non-unit z then needs
   442	  exact gcd layers, and FM needs a new scalar-kernel lower bound, because the offset is as large as the
   443	  window (`notes/ldbound_codex1_report.md` §§4–5). The prime w, kept polynomially below H/M^{d−1}, avoids
   444	  both.
