   105	## 1. Design (a″_k): a two-dimensional random rational side
   106	
   107	Everything not mentioned here is as in hypD §1–§3: Dixon descent, Theorem DD, Theorems S/S′ and K, the
   108	field-selection rule and the LP shape test. Those results treat the field A abstractly and act only on
   109	R and A, so they apply verbatim to the field law below.
   110	
   111	**Notation.**
   112	- L = L_N[1/2, 1] and Λ = (log N log log N)^{1/2}.
   113	- s = 1/(d+1), M = ⌊N^s⌋, B = L[1/2, a], ϱ(x, y) = Ψ(x, y)/x, and 𝒮_B = the B-smooth integers (sign
   114	  ignored).
   115	- ℓ is uniform among the primes of J = (2^{j−1}, 2^j], with 2^{kj} ≍ √N, so ℓ = N^{1/(2k)+o(1)}.
   116	- S = S(N) = L^{σ+o(1)}, with σ in a fixed compact subinterval of (0, ∞), and V = ⌊√ℓ·S⌋.
   117	  - S is common to all ℓ ∈ J. Theorem 4.3 takes S² = (log N)^{C₃}/(ρ_rρ_F), with ρ_r, ρ_F evaluated at
   118	    the dyadic envelope V_max = √(2Y)·S; this gives σ = θ/(4a).
   119	  - All statements below are uniform in such sequences: every polynomial margin and auxiliary exponent
   120	    depends only on the compact σ-range (codex pcest_3).
   121	  - Equalities involving V are up to bounded factors (floors).
   122	- H = N^h.
   123	
   124	**Preprocessing [standard].** Trial division up to B, at cost B·(log N)^{O(1)} = L^{a+o(1)}. This is
   125	below the final cost L^{c}, since c ≥ 2a. Afterwards N has no prime factor ≤ B, and is not a prime
   126	power. This replaces Lemma Ω for everything below; 2^ω = L^{o(1)} still holds.
   127	
   128	**Field law 𝒜.**
   129	- (g₀, g₁) is uniform on 𝒢 := {(g₀, g₁) ∈ Z² ∩ [M/2, M]² : gcd(g₀, g₁) = 1}, and G(x, y) := g₁x − g₀y.
   130	- c is uniform on C_H := [−H, H]^d ∩ Z^d, and R_c(x, y) := Σ_{i<d} c_i x^{d−1−i}y^i.
   131	- F_A := F̂_g + G·R_c, with F̂_g from Lemma GB. For P = (a, b):
   132	    F_A(P) = F̂_g(P) + G(P)·⟨c, v(P)⟩,  v(P) := (a^{d−1−i}b^i)_{0≤i<d}.
   133	- If gcd(g₁, N) > 1 or gcd(G(P), N) > 1, a factor of N has been found. Otherwise put
   134	  ψ: Z[x] → Z/N, x ↦ g₀g₁^{−1}. It is well defined on Z[x]/(f): g₁^d f(g₀/g₁) = F(g₀, g₁) = N ≡ 0
   135	  (mod N).
   136	- F_A(P) = 0 is excluded from the counts.
   137	
   138	**Lemma GB (generalized base expansion) [proved].** Let g₁ ≥ 2 and gcd(g₀, g₁) = 1. Define
   139	T_d := N, and for i = d, d−1, …, 1:
   140	  a_i ≡ T_i g₀^{−i} (mod g₁), |a_i| ≤ g₁/2,  T_{i−1} := (T_i − a_i g₀^i)/g₁,
   141	and finally a_0 := T_0. Then F̂(g₀, g₁) := Σ_i a_i g₀^i g₁^{d−i} = N. If g₀, g₁ ∈ [M/2, M], then
   142	|a_0| ≤ 2^{d+1}M + d2^{d−1}M, so ‖f̂‖_∞ = O_d(M).
   143	*Proof.*
   144	1. T_i ≡ a_i g₀^i (mod g₁) by the choice of a_i, so T_{i−1} ∈ Z.
   145	2. By induction, T_i = Σ_{j≤i} a_j g₀^j g₁^{i−j}. At i = d this is the claim.
   146	3. For the bound, a_0 = (N − Σ_{i≥1} a_i g₀^i g₁^{d−i})/g₁^d. Here N/g₁^d ≤ 2^d N/M^d ≤ 2^{d+1}M, and
   147	   each |a_i|(g₀/g₁)^i ≤ (g₁/2)·2^i ≤ 2^{i−1}M. ∎
   148	(`src/pcest/geometry.py: gbe`; tested.)
   149	
   150	*Remarks.*
   151	- **Prior art.** The non-monic linear side g₁x − g₀ with a base-(g₀, g₁) expansion is Buhler–Lenstra–
   152	  Pomerance's base-(m₁, m₂) variant (LNM 1554 (1993) 50–94; Murphy, thesis, ANU 1999, §2.3.2). Lemma GB
   153	  is that construction with explicit bounds. The design is not new. What we did not find elsewhere is
   154	  its use to randomize the rational side in a rigorous pair-correlation argument (Theorem R).
   155	- **Kleinjung constraint.** Kleinjung (Math. Comp. 75, 2006) prescribes a small leading coefficient
   156	  a_d, and therefore needs a_d g₀^d ≡ N (mod g₁). Here a_d is *determined* by (g₀, g₁), so (g₀, g₁)
   157	  can be sampled freely subject only to coprimality. Codex makes the same point.
   158	- **Sizes.** The price is a_d ≈ g₁ rather than a small leading coefficient. That is irrelevant at the
   159	  exponent level, because LV's f̂ already has coefficients ≈ M.
   160	
   161	**Lemma 1.2 [proved].** For every P = (a, b), g₁^d F_A(P) ≡ N b^d (mod G(P)).
   162	*Proof.* g₁·P − b·(g₀, g₁) = (G(P), 0). Since F_A is an integral form, g₁^d F_A(P) = F_A(g₁P) ≡
   163	F_A(b·(g₀, g₁)) = b^d F_A(g₀, g₁) = b^d N, because G(g₀, g₁) = 0. ∎
   164	
   165	**Relations.** If G(P) = ±ℓn with n ∈ 𝒮_B and F_A(P) ∈ 𝒮_B, then
   166	  ψ(a − bα) = G(P)/g₁ = ±ℓn/g₁ (mod N).
   167	This is hypD's descent relation, multiplied by the per-field unit g₁^{−1}.
   168	- One extra column, the parity of the number of relations, makes g₁ a square in every dependency.
   169	  This is the same device LV and Buhler–Lenstra–Pomerance use for f_d.
   170	- **Irreducibility.** LV Lemma 5.1 transfers. Its Turán-sieve count uses only the affine structure
   171	  f ∈ f̂ + (x − m)·Z[x]_{d−1}, and the condition "g(m) ≡ n (mod r)" for the reductions g mod r. With
   172	  g₁x − g₀ in place of x − m, the condition becomes ĝ(g₀, g₁) ≡ N (mod r) for r ∤ g₁N. Claim 5.2
   173	  counts monic irreducibles with a prescribed value at an affine point m, here m = g₀g₁^{−1} mod r
   174	  (r ∤ g₁). It is unchanged.
   175	  [transfer of a published proof, checked at the level of its statements]
   176	  - a_d ≠ 0 in Lemma GB, because a_d ≡ N g₀^{−d} (mod g₁) and gcd(g₁, N) = 1.
   177	- A reducible f either gives F₁(g₀, g₁)F₂(g₀, g₁) = N with both factors ≠ ±1, which is a factorization,
   178	  or is rare.
   179	- **Quantified (Opus proof critic).** LV's bound transplants as Pr(f reducible) ≤ H^{−1/3+o(1)}. Field
   180	  selection keeps a field with law ∝ q_A, which amplifies rare events by at most 1/μ. So
   181	  Pr(kept field reducible) ≤ H^{−1/3+o(1)}/μ = N^{−h/3+o(1)}. The degree-drop event
   182	  a_d + g₁c₀ = 0 has probability ≤ 1/(2H + 1). Reducible fields are rejected.
   183	  - Status: transfer of LV Lemma 5.1, checked at the level of statements. It is listed among the
   184	    assumptions of Theorem A.
   185	
   186	**Counted candidates and the capped search.**
   187	- Put 𝒫_ℓ := {(a, b) : b prime, V/2 < b ≤ V, |a| ≤ V, gcd(a, b) = 1}, and
   188	  Λ_ℓ(g) := {P : ℓ | G(P)}, which has index ℓ when ℓ ∤ g₁.
   189	- The search enumerates Λ_ℓ(g) ∩ [−V, V]² from a Gauss-reduced basis, tests membership in 𝒫_ℓ, and
   190	  tests G(P)/ℓ and F_A(P) for B-smoothness.
   191	  - Primality of b uses a deterministic polynomial-time test (AKS).
   192	  - Smoothness is tested by HECM, as in LV Cor. 3.19.
   193	- N_ℓ(A) := #{P ∈ 𝒫_ℓ ∩ Λ_ℓ(g) : |G(P)| ≥ MV/32, G(P)/ℓ ∈ 𝒮_B, F_A(P) ∈ 𝒮_B}. Each such P is a
   194	  certified descent relation. ℓ² ∤ G(P) holds automatically, because ℓ > B.
   195	  - The **filter |G(P)| ≥ MV/32** was added after codex pcest_2 (§7.2). Without it, candidates with
   196	    |G| as small as ℓ have algebraic values smaller by a polynomial factor. Their smoothness density
   197	    can then exceed ρ_F by L^{Θ(1)}, which breaks Theorem 4.3's comparison. FM only ever uses
   198	    |G| ∈ [MV/32, 2MV], so the filter costs nothing there.
   199	  - A capped randomized smoothness test is repeated O(log N) times, so that a smooth value is
   200	    recognized with probability ≥ 1 − N^{−2}. This costs a polylogarithmic factor.
   201	- **Lemma 1.3 (regularity) [proved].** Suppose λ₁(Λ_ℓ(g)) < √ℓ/(2S), attained at w. Then
   202	  Λ_ℓ(g) ∩ 𝒫_ℓ = ∅. Otherwise #(Λ_ℓ(g) ∩ [−V, V]²) ≤ C_abs·S² for an absolute constant C_abs (e.g. 100).
   203	  *Proof.*
   204	  - w is primitive in Z²: its content e satisfies e ≤ |w| < ℓ, so gcd(e, ℓ) = 1 and w/e ∈ Λ_ℓ(g); by
   205	    minimality e = 1.
   206	  - So w is primitive in Λ_ℓ(g) (index ℓ), and the lattice is the union of the lines jw′ + Zw, j ∈ Z,
   207	    which are parallel to w at mutual distance ℓ/|w| > 2V.
   208	  - Every point of [−V, V]² has norm ≤ √2V < 2V, so it lies on the central line Zw. The only primitive
   209	    points there are ±w, and |w| < V/2 < b for every point of 𝒫_ℓ.
   210	  - In the regular case take a reduced basis (λ₁ ≤ λ₂, λ₁λ₂ ≤ (2/√3)ℓ). The translates of the
   211	    fundamental cell F at the lattice points of K = [−V, V]² are disjoint and lie in K + F. So the count
   212	    is ≤ (area(K) + perimeter(K)·diam F + π diam(F)²)/ℓ ≤ 4S² + O(V/λ₁) + O(λ₂/λ₁) ≤ C_abs S², using
   213	    λ₁ ≥ √ℓ/(2S), V/λ₁ ≤ 2S² and λ₂/λ₁ ≤ (2/√3)ℓ/λ₁² ≤ 8S²/√3. ∎
   214	- So the cap T_SQ := C_abs S²·(log N)^{O(1)} is never binding on a lattice that contains a candidate.
   215	  Irregular lattices need no separate exclusion: they contribute nothing.
   216	
   217	**Sizes [proved arithmetic].** For P ∈ 𝒫_ℓ and g ∈ 𝒢:
   218	- |G(P)| ≤ 2MV, so |G/ℓ| ≤ X_r := 2MV/ℓ = N^{s−1/(4k)}L^{O(1)};
   219	- |F̂_g(P)| = O_d(MV^d);
   220	- |G(P)⟨c, v(P)⟩| ≤ 2dMHV^d, so |F_A(P)| ≤ X_F := C_d MHV^d = N^{s+h+d/(4k)}L^{O(1)}.
   221	
   222	With ρ_r := ϱ(X_r, B) and ρ_F := ϱ(X_F, B), these are exactly hypD's exponents, so
   223	θ = 2s + h + (d−1)/(4k) as in `src/pcest/params.py`.
