   420	
   421	## 3. PC_rat: Theorem R
   422	
   423	Throughout this section Y_P := 1[G(P)/ℓ ∈ 𝒮_B] is the rational event for a candidate P. The rational
   424	counts are
   425	  R1 := E_{ℓ,g} Σ_{P∈𝒫_ℓ} 1[P ∈ Λ_ℓ(g)]Y_P,
   426	  R2 := E_{ℓ,g} Σ_{P≠P′∈𝒫_ℓ} 1[P, P′ ∈ Λ_ℓ(g)]Y_PY_{P′}.
   427	
   428	**Lemma Q (pair image lattice) [proved; tested].**
   429	- Let P = (a, b) ≠ P′ = (a′, b′) be in 𝒫_ℓ with Δ := ab′ − a′b ≡ 0 (mod ℓ). Δ ≠ 0, since b, b′ > 0 and
   430	  both points are primitive.
   431	- Then L_ℓ(P) := {g ∈ Z² : ℓ | G_g(P)} = L_ℓ(P′).
   432	- The map g ↦ (n, n′) := (G_g(P)/ℓ, G_g(P′)/ℓ) is injective on L_ℓ(P), and its image is
   433	    Γ_q = {(n, n′) ∈ Z² : n′ ≡ λn (mod q)},  q := |Δ|/ℓ ≤ 2S²,  λ :≡ b′b^{−1} (mod q).
   434	- Since b is a prime > V/2 > 2S² ≥ q, b is invertible mod q, and λ is a unit.
   435	
   436	*Proof.*
   437	0. Pairs with ℓ ∤ Δ contribute nothing. Since g is primitive, its reduction mod ℓ is nonzero, and two
   438	   vectors annihilated by the nonzero linear form x ↦ g₁x₁ − g₀x₂ mod ℓ have determinant ≡ 0 (mod ℓ).
   439	1. g ↦ (G_g(P), G_g(P′)) is the integer matrix [[a, −b], [a′, −b′]] acting on (g₁, g₀). Its determinant
   440	   is −Δ ≠ 0, so the map is injective with image of index |Δ|.
   441	2. L_ℓ(P) has index ℓ in Z². ℓ | Δ forces ℓ | G_g(P′) whenever ℓ | G_g(P), so L_ℓ(P) = L_ℓ(P′), and its
   442	   image lies in ℓZ² with index ℓ|Δ| in Z². Dividing by ℓ gives a lattice of index ℓ|Δ|/ℓ² = q.
   443	3. b·G_g(P′) − b′·G_g(P) = g₁(ba′ − b′a) = −g₁Δ, so bn′ − b′n = ∓g₁q ≡ 0 (mod q). The image therefore
   444	   lies in Γ_q, which also has index q, so the two are equal. ∎
   445	- This matches codex pcest_1 (3). The index is q, not ℓq: the special-q condition is absorbed by the
   446	  division by ℓ.
   447	
   448	**Lemma DS (divisor switching: smooth numbers in a short interval and a progression) [proved;
   449	elementary].**
   450	- Setup: let C₀ ≥ 1 be fixed, y → ∞ with y ≥ (log X)^K, u = log X/log y → ∞, and 1 ≤ q ≤ y^{C₀}. Let
   451	  I ⊂ [X y^{−C₀}, X] be an interval of length A ≥ X y^{−C₀}.
   452	- Conclusion: for every r ∈ Z,
   453	    #{n ∈ I : n ≡ r (mod q), P⁺(n) ≤ y} ≤ (A/q)·ϱ(X, y)·(log X)^{O_{C₀}(1)}.
   454	
   455	*Proof.*
   456	1. Let e := gcd(r, q). If e has a prime factor > y, the count is 0. Otherwise write n = en₁. Then
   457	   n₁ ∈ I/e has length A/e and n₁ ≡ r/e (mod q′) with q′ = q/e and gcd(r/e, q′) = 1, so n₁ is coprime
   458	   to q′.
   459	2. Put D := min(A/q, X/(e y^{C₀})). Then D ≤ min(I/e).
   460	3. Every y-smooth n₁ ≥ D has a divisor d with D/y < d ≤ D: take the largest product of its smallest
   461	   prime factors that is ≤ D.
   462	4. Write n₁ = dm. Then gcd(d, q′) = 1, m ≡ (r/e)d^{−1} (mod q′), and m lies in an interval of length
   463	   A/(ed). So there are ≤ A/(dq) + 1 ≤ 2A/(dq) choices of m, since A/(Dq) ≥ 1.
   464	5. Hence the count is ≤ (2A/q) Σ_{D/y<d≤D, P⁺(d)≤y} 1/d.
   465	6. By dyadic blocks and (Hi), the sum is ≤ 8 log₂(2y)·ϱ(D/y, y).
   466	7. Finally X/(D/y) ≤ y^{2C₀+1}, so (C8.16) gives ϱ(D/y, y) ≤ ϱ(X, y)(log X)^{O(C₀)}. ∎
   467	*Attribution.*
   468	- The case q = 1 follows from Hildebrand's subadditivity Ψ(x + z, y) ≤ Ψ(x, y) + Ψ(z, y) (Quart. J.
   469	  Math. 36 (1985) 57–69) together with (C8.16).
   470	- Choosing a divisor in (D/y, D] is the standard flexible factorization of smooth numbers (Friedlander,
   471	  Acta Arith. 39 (1981); Vaughan's iterative method).
   472	- Codex pcest_2 points to Harper, Compositio 152 (2016), §2.1, "Smooth Numbers Result 3", as a stronger
   473	  published progression version, including non-coprime residues. We have not checked its exact ranges
   474	  against ours, so we use our own proof above.
   475	- Lemma DS is therefore a progression extension of known short-interval bounds, not a new result.
   476	- Codex's blind round states the same bound (its (1)).
   477	
   478	**Theorem R (rational pair correlation) [proved].** In design (a″_k), with B = L[1/2, a] and S = L^{σ+o(1)}
   479	satisfying S² ≥ ρ_r^{−2}:
   480	  R2 ≤ (log N)^{O(1)}·R1²  and  R1 ≥ S²ρ_r/(log N)^{O(1)}.
   481	
   482	*Proof.*
   483	- **The lower bound on R1** is Steps 1 and 4 of §2.3 without the algebraic factor.
   484	- **The pair count.** Fix ℓ and a pair (P, P′) as in Lemma Q. Then
   485	    #{g ∈ 𝒢 ∩ L_ℓ(P) : Y_PY_{P′} = 1} ≤ #{(n, n′) ∈ Γ_q ∩ Π : n, n′ ∈ 𝒮_B}.
   486	  Here Π := ℓ^{−1}[[a, −b], [a′, −b′]]·[M/2, M]² is a parallelogram of area M²q/(4ℓ), contained in
   487	  [−X_r, X_r]².
   488	- **Shape of Π.** One side is (M/2ℓ)(b, b′), of length ≍ MV/ℓ and with direction in
   489	  [atan(1/2), atan 2], because b, b′ ∈ (V/2, V]. So Π has width ≥ area/(2·long side) ≥ Mq/(8√2V) ≥
   490	  w₀/12, where w₀ := M/V.
   491	- **Covering.** Cover Π by the grid squares of side w₀ that meet it. Their number is
   492	  ≪ area(Π)/w₀² + perimeter(Π)/w₀ + 1 ≪ qS² + S² + 1 ≪ qS²; this absorbs boundary squares.
   493	- **A square away from the axes.** Let 𝒬 = I₁ × I₂ have |n|, |n′| ≥ X_r S^{−2} throughout.
   494	  - Lemma DS applies to each coordinate with X = 2X_r. Negative intervals are reflected, with the
   495	    residue adjusted. I_i ⊂ [X_rS^{−2}, 2X_r], A = w₀ = X_r/(2S²), and q ≤ 2S². Choose a fixed
   496	    C₀ > max(1, θ/(2a²)), so that B^{C₀} ≥ 4S², since S² = L^{θ/(2a)+o(1)}. Non-reduced residues are
   497	    included.
   498	  - Hence #(Γ_q ∩ 𝒬 ∩ 𝒮_B²) = Σ_{r mod q} #{n ∈ I₁ : n ≡ r}·#{n′ ∈ I₂ : n′ ≡ λr}
   499	    ≤ q·((w₀/q)ρ_r(log)^{O(1)})² = (w₀²/q)ρ_r²(log)^{O(1)}.
   500	  - Summing over the ≤ CqS² squares gives ≤ CS²w₀²ρ_r²(log)^{O(1)} = C(M²/ℓ)ρ_r²(log)^{O(1)}.
   501	- **Near the axes.** For a square that meets the band |n| < X_rS^{−2}, count only its points that lie
   502	  in Π (the original points). They have |n| < 2X_rS^{−2}, since w₀ = X_r/(2S²). Each n-value has
   503	  ≤ √2M/V + 1 preimages g (Step 1(i)). So there are at most 8MX_r/(VS²) = 16M²/(ℓS²) such points; the
   504	  same holds for n′.
   505	  - For the filtered count of §1 (|G| ≥ MV/32, i.e. |n| ≥ X_r/64), this band is empty.
   506	  - Squares crossing the boundary of [−X_r, X_r]² are handled by applying Lemma DS with X = 2X_r.
   507	  - With the choice S² = (log N)^{C₃}/(ρ_rρ_F) of Theorem 4.3: ρ_F/ρ_r = L^{−[h+(d+1)/(4k)]/(2a)+o(1)},
   508	    so S^{−2} ≤ ρ_r² for large N, and this term is absorbed. In general Theorem R needs S² ≥ ρ_r^{−2}.
   509	- **Per pair**, the count is ≤ (M²/ℓ)ρ_r²(log N)^{O(1)}. This bound is independent of q.
   510	- **Counting pairs.** For fixed P, the P′ with ℓ | Δ are the points of Λ_ℓ(g) ∩ 𝒫_ℓ for g = (a, b),
   511	  i.e. g₀/g₁ ≡ a/b. This lattice contains P ∈ 𝒫_ℓ, so it is regular. There are
   512	  ≤ C_abs S² of them (Lemma 1.3). Hence
   513	    R2(ℓ) ≤ #𝒫_ℓ·C_abs S²·|𝒢|^{−1}(M²/ℓ)ρ_r²(log)^{O(1)} ≍ (V²/log V)(S²/ℓ)ρ_r²(log)^{O(1)}
   514	          = S⁴ρ_r²(log)^{O(1)}.
   515	- **Conclusion.** By the lower bound, R1(ℓ) ≥ S²ρ_r/(log)^{O(1)} for every ℓ ∈ J.
   516	  - Across the dyadic interval J, the density arguments X_r, X_F differ by bounded factors. So ρ_r and
   517	    ρ_F, and with them both bounds, are uniform in ℓ up to polylogarithms. Averaging over ℓ then proves
   518	    the theorem.
   519	  - For the filtered count this gives R2,filt(ℓ) ≤ R2(ℓ) ≪ S⁴ρ_r²(log)^{O(1)} and
   520	    R1,filt(ℓ) ≫ S²ρ_r/(log)^{O(1)} directly. Monotonicity of the ratio under filtering is not used. ∎

