   243	- **(T) Tenenbaum (LV Fact 3.16, Cor. 3.17).** If ω(t) ≤ y^c/log(1 + u), then
   244	    Ψ_t(x, y) = (φ(t)/t)Ψ(x, y)(1 + O(log(1+u) log(1+ω(t))/log y))(1 + O(ω(t)/y)).
   245	  In our range the error is o(1) uniformly for t ≤ x, since ω(t) ≤ log x.
   246	- **(Hi) Hildebrand.**
   247	  - LV Fact 3.13: for log y ≥ (log log x)^{5/3+ε} and 1 ≤ z ≤ y^{5/12},
   248	      Ψ(x(1 + 1/z), y) − Ψ(x, y) = (Ψ(x, y)/z)(1 + O(log(u+1)/log y)).
   249	  - Ψ(2x, y) ≤ 2Ψ(x, y) (LV ref. [19, Thm 4]). Hence ϱ(z₂) ≤ 2ϱ(z₁) whenever z₂ ≥ z₁.
   250	- **(C8.16) LV Claim 8.16.** If u → ∞ and c ≥ 0 is fixed, then ϱ(x, y) = ϱ(xy^c, y)(log x)^{O(⌈c⌉)}.
   251	  So ϱ changes by (log N)^{O(1)} = L^{o(1)} when its argument moves by a factor y^{O(1)} = L^{O(1)}.
   252	- **(LV5) LV Lemmas 5.13, 5.18, 5.20.** These concern only the linear map φ_P(c) = ⟨c, v(P)⟩. They do
   253	  not involve m, so they transplant verbatim (§2.3, step 2).
   254	
   255	### 2.2 Lemma 8.5′ (restricted BV for moduli ℓ·(smooth)) [proved; regime (B)]
   256	
   257	**Statement.**
   258	- Let 0 < q < ξ/2 with q > 1/(2k) + η₁, for some fixed η₁ > 0, and Q = N^{q+o(1)}.
   259	- Let Y = 2^{j−1} and 𝒯_Q := {t = ℓs ∈ (Q/2, Q] : ℓ ∈ (Y, 2Y] prime, P⁺(s) ≤ B}.
   260	- Let δ_𝒯 := ϱ(Q/Y, B)/log Y. Then #𝒯_Q ≍ Qδ_𝒯.
   261	- Conclusion: Σ_{t∈𝒯_Q} max_{(r,t)=1} |Ψ(x, B; t, r) − Ψ_t(x, B)/φ(t)| ≪ Ψ(x, B)δ_𝒯·ε_N, where
   262	  ε_N := H(u)^{−c} + B^{−c} + x^{−η₂} for some fixed η₂ > 0.
   263	
   264	*Proof.*
   265	- **Setup.**
   266	  - #𝒯_Q = Σ_ℓ (Ψ(Q/ℓ) − Ψ(Q/2ℓ)).
   267	  - Fact 3.15 gives Ψ(z/2) = 2^{−α}Ψ(z)(1 + o(1)) with α ≥ 1/2, so each difference is ≍ Ψ(Q/ℓ).
   268	  - Summing gives #𝒯_Q ≍ Qδ_𝒯.
   269	  - Since ℓ > B ≥ P⁺(s), ℓ ∤ s and φ(t) = (ℓ − 1)φ(s). Every character mod t is χ_ℓχ_s, and t
   270	    determines (ℓ, s).
   271	  - The left side is at most W′ := Σ_{t∈𝒯_Q} φ(t)^{−1} Σ_{χ≠χ₀ mod t} |Ψ(x, B; χ)|.
   272	- **Part 1: χ_ℓ principal, χ_s ≠ χ₀.**
   273	  - Every B-smooth n is coprime to ℓ, so Ψ(x, B; χ₀^{(ℓ)}χ_s) = Ψ(x, B; χ_s).
   274	  - So this part equals Σ_ℓ (ℓ−1)^{−1} Σ_{s∈(Q/2ℓ, Q/ℓ], smooth} φ(s)^{−1} Σ_{χ_s≠χ₀} |Ψ(x, B; χ_s)|.
   275	  - By positivity this is ≤ Σ_ℓ (ℓ−1)^{−1} W(Q/ℓ), with ω₀ = log x.
   276	  - (LV8.5) applies with Q′ = Q/ℓ = N^{q−1/(2k)+o(1)} ≥ x^ε, where ε := η₁/(2ξ), and Q′ ≤ √Ψ.
   277	  - Hence Part 1 ≪ Ψ(H(u)^{−c} + B^{−c})·Σ_ℓ ϱ(Q/ℓ)/ℓ + Q√Ψ log^{7/2}x·Σ_ℓ ℓ^{−2}.
   278	  - The first sum is ≤ 2ϱ(Q/2Y)Σ_ℓ ℓ^{−1} ≪ δ_𝒯, using (Hi) and Mertens on (Y, 2Y].
   279	  - The second term is ≪ (Q/Y)√Ψ log^{7/2}x ≤ Ψδ_𝒯x^{−η₂}. This holds because Q ≤ x^{1/2−(ξ/2−q)/2ξ}
   280	    and Ψ, δ_𝒯 ≥ x^{−o(1)}.
   281	- **Part 2: χ_ℓ non-principal.**
   282	  - The conductor is ℓ·r′ ≥ Y = N^{1/(2k)+o(1)}.
   283	  - Choose η := min(1/100, 1/(4kξ)). Then Y ≥ x^η = M₀, so all these characters have conductor in
   284	    [M₀, Q], with moduli t ∈ (Q/2, Q] ⊂ [M₀, Q].
   285	  - By positivity, Part 2 is at most the left side of Fact 8.11, which is
   286	      ≪ log^{7/2}x √Ψ (Q + x^{1/2−η} log²x) ≤ Ψδ_𝒯 x^{−η₂},
   287	    using q < ξ/2 and Ψ, δ_𝒯 ≥ x^{−o(1)}.
   288	- The principal character of t contributes exactly Ψ_t(x)/φ(t), which is the subtracted main term. ∎
   289	
   290	*Remarks.*
   291	- Unlike hypD §4.1′, no conductor range [Y, M₀] arises in regime (B), so neither Fact 8.9 nor Fact 8.10
   292	  is needed for χ_ℓ ≠ χ₀.
   293	- Inherited from LV's proof: the Page-character step absorbs (log x)^{O(1)} into exp(−cu/log²u), which
   294	  needs u/log²u ≫ log log x. That holds in regime (B), where u ≍ (log N/log log N)^{1/2}, but not at
   295	  the edge of LV's stated range. We use Lemma 8.5′ only in regime (B).
   296	- In the setup, Fact 3.15 is used with α → 1. Ψ(2x, y) ≤ 2Ψ(x, y) can equally be taken from Fact 3.15,
   297	  up to a factor 1 + o(1).
   298	- ε_N → 0 uniformly in N, and ε_N·(log L)^{O(1)} → 0, since H(u)^{−c} = exp(−c(log N)^{1/2}/
   299	  (log log N)^{5/2+o(1)}).
   300	
   301	### 2.3 Theorem FM [proved]
   302	
   303	**Statement.**
   304	- Fix odd d ≥ 3, k > (d+1)/4, a > 0, a compact σ-range ⊂ (0, ∞) for S = L^{σ+o(1)}, and η₀ > 0. Let
   305	  h ≥ max(1/(4k), s − (d−2)/(4k)) + η₀.
   306	- Then for all large N (preprocessed), with an implied constant depending only on (d, k, a, σ, h, η₀):
   307	    E_{ℓ∈J, A∼𝒜} N_ℓ(A) ≥ S²ρ_rρ_F/(log N)^{O(1)}.
   308	
   309	*Proof.* Write Pr_A for g uniform on 𝒢 and c uniform on C_H, independently. Then
   310	  E N ≥ π_J^{−1} Σ_{ℓ∈J} Σ_{P∈𝒫_ℓ} Σ_{n∈I_P∩𝒮_B} (|𝒢_P(n)|/|𝒢|)·E_{g∈𝒢_P(n)} Pr_c[F_A(P) ∈ 𝒮_B],
   311	with I_P from Step 1(ii). This is an inequality: the filter |G| ≥ MV/32 holds on I_P, and the other
   312	n are dropped.
   313	where 𝒢_P(n) := {g ∈ 𝒢 : G_g(P) = ℓn}. Throughout, (log)^{O(1)} denotes (log N)^{O(1)}.
   314	
   315	**Step 1 (fibres and the induced law; hypD item 2).** Fix ℓ and P = (a, b) ∈ 𝒫_ℓ, and put V′ :=
   316	max(|a|, b) ∈ (V/2, V].
   317	- (i) The g with G_g(P) = ℓn lie on a line with direction (a, b) and point spacing |P|₂ ≥ V/2. The
   318	  square has chords of length ≤ M/√2. So |𝒢_P(n)| ≤ √2M/V + 1 for every n.
   319	- (ii) Consider the continuous model (g₀, g₁) uniform on [M/2, M]².
   320	  - G = g₁a − g₀b is a sum of independent uniforms on intervals of lengths |a|M/2 and bM/2.
   321	  - Its density is ≥ 1/(MV′) on a central interval of length ≥ MV′/2 ≥ MV/4.
   322	  - Removing |G| < MV/32 leaves an interval I*_P of G-values, of length ≥ MV/16, on which
   323	    |G| ∈ [MV/32, 2MV] and G has constant sign.
   324	  - For t ∈ I*_P the chord {G = t} has length ≥ (M²/4)|P|₂/(MV′) ≥ M/4, so it carries ≥ M/(8V)
   325	    lattice points for large N.
   326	  - Put I_P := I*_P/ℓ, a set of n-values of length ≥ MV/(16ℓ).
   327	- (iii) **Coprimality.** The integer points of the line g = g* + j(a, b) on which e | gcd(g₀, g₁) form
   328	  one residue class of j mod e if e | ℓn, and none otherwise, because gcd(a, b) = 1. By Möbius
   329	  inversion, for n ∈ I_P,
   330	    |𝒢_P(n)| ≥ (φ(ℓn)/ℓn)·M/(8V) − 2^{ω(ℓn)} ≥ M/(C V log log N),
   331	  since 2^{ω(ℓn)} ≤ N^{o(1)} ≪ M/V.
   332	
   333	**Step 2 (the algebraic side; hypD items 3, 4).** Fix ℓ, P, n ∈ I_P ∩ 𝒮_B and g ∈ 𝒢_P(n). Put
   334	t := |G(P)| = ℓ|n| and r := F̂_g(P) mod t.
   335	- (a) **(r, t) = 1, so the moving residue is reduced (item 3).**
   336	  - gcd(G, b) = gcd(g₁, b), and g₁a ≡ ℓn (mod b). Since b is a prime > B ≥ P⁺(n) and b ≠ ℓ (b ≤ V < ℓ),
   337	    gcd(g₁, b) = 1.
   338	  - gcd(G, g₁) = gcd(g₀b, g₁) = 1.
   339	  - Lemma 1.2 then gives gcd(r, t) = gcd(N, t) = 1, by preprocessing and ℓ ∤ N.
   340	  - The residue r moves with g and b. It enters only through max_r below.
   341	- (b) **Kernel and ϑ (LV 5.13, 5.18; item 4).**
   342	  - LV's induction in Lemma 5.13 uses only gcd(a, b) = 1. Exchanging the roles of a and b when
   343	    |a| > b, and absorbing signs into the x_i, it gives S_P ⊂ [−2V′, 2V′]^d on which φ_P is a
   344	    bijection onto an interval of length V′^{d−1}. It also gives the sub-kernels ν_l with
   345	    φ_Pν_l = Unif(I(l)).
   346	  - As in Lemma 5.18, with μ = Unif(C_H) and ν = μ ⋆ (⋆_i ν_{l_i}), l_i = |a|^{d−1−i}b^i:
   347	    ‖μ − ν‖₁ ≤ 2·Pr_μ[dist(c, ∂C_H) ≤ 4dV′] ≤ 8d²V/H.
   348	  - Also φ_Pν = ϑ, a convolution of d uniforms on intervals of lengths 2Hl_i, shifted by
   349	    |M₀| ≤ dV^{d−1}.
   350	  - Its density is ≤ (2H max_i l_i)^{−1} = (2HV′^{d−1})^{−1}, and its support lies in
   351	    [M₀ − T′, M₀ + T′] with T′ ≤ dHV^{d−1}.
   352	- (c) **Truncation (LV 5.20; item 4).** Fix ω := L^{C₁}, where C₁ exceeds the exponent of 1/ρ_F.
   353	  - Put Y_ω := (2H + 1)V′^{d−1}/ω. Let ϑ′ be LV's **flattening** of ϑ on the central part:
   354	    ϑ′(z) = ϑ(max(z, Y_ω)) for z ≥ 0 and ϑ(min(z, −Y_ω)) for z < 0. Nothing is deleted. Discrete
   355	    interval lengths are 2H + 1 rather than 2H; this changes only constants.
   356	  - Then ‖ϑ − ϑ′‖₁ ≤ 2/ω.
   357	  - ϑ′ is monotone away from 0, since Y_ω > |M₀| when H > dωV. So ϑ′ = Σ_{y≥Y_ω} (W_y Unif[0, y] +
   358	    W_{−y} Unif[−y, 0)), with W ≥ 0 and |1 − ΣW| ≤ 2/ω.
   359	- (d) **One uniform piece.**
   360	  - Let y ∈ [Y_ω, 2T′]. The values F̂ + G·m, m ∈ [0, y], form a progression ≡ r (mod t).
   361	  - **Sign correction (codex pcest_2).** If G and F̂ have opposite signs, the progression can cross 0;
   362	    its far endpoint then has absolute value ty − |F̂|, not ty. In every case, all terms except at most
   363	    2(|F̂|/t + 1) of them have one sign and absolute value in (|F̂|, ty − |F̂|]. This interval contains
   364	    (ty/4, ty/2], because |F̂| ≤ ty/4.
   365	  - Let X_F^max := C_d MH V_max^d with V_max := ⌊√(2Y)·S⌋, so it is common to all ℓ ∈ J. Let
   366	    𝒳 := {X_F^max ω^{−2}(1 + 1/2)^i} ∩ [X_F^max ω^{−2}, 16dX_F^max].
   367	    - 𝒳 has O(log ω) = O(Λ) elements.
   368	    - It is independent of t, P, ℓ and the residue.
   369	    - It contains a point x₁ ∈ (ty/4, ty/2]. Consecutive points differ by the factor 3/2 < 2, and
   370	      ty/4 ≥ tY_ω/4 ≥ X_F^max ω^{−2} for large N, since tY_ω ≥ X_F^max/(C_d 2^{2d}ω).
   371	  - Call t **good** if, for all x ∈ 𝒳 and all reduced residues r′,
   372	    |Ψ(x, B; t, r′) − Ψ_t(x, B)/φ(t)| ≤ Ψ_t(x, B)/(2φ(t)).
   373	  - For good t, (T) and (Hi) give
   374	      #{smooth terms} ≥ Ψ(x₁; t, ±r) − Ψ(|F̂|; t, ±r) − 2(|F̂|/t + 1)
   375	                      ≥ Ψ(x₁)/(4t) − 3(|F̂|/t + 1) ≥ Ψ(ty)/(16t) − 3(|F̂|/t + 1).
   376	      (The progression contains every integer ≡ ±r (mod t) with absolute value in (|F̂|, x₁].)
   377	  - Dividing by y + 1 and using ϱ(ty) ≥ ϱ(16dX_F^max)/2 ≥ ρ_F/(log)^{O(1)} (by (Hi) and (C8.16)) gives
   378	    Pr ≥ ρ_F/(log)^{O(1)} − O(ωV/H). The error term is polynomially small, since |F̂|/(tY_ω) ≪ ωV/H.
   379	- Combining (b)–(d): **if t is good, then Pr_c[F_A(P) ∈ 𝒮_B] ≥ ρ_F/(log)^{O(1)} − 8d²V/H − 4/ω ≥
   380	  ρ_F/(log)^{O(1)}.**
   381	  - Here V/H ≤ N^{−η₀}L^{O(1)} by the h-floor 1/(4k) + η₀, and 1/ω = L^{−C₁}.
   382	  - This is hypD item 4: additive errors ≪ ρ_F rather than o(1), with truncation ω ≥ L^C.
   383	
   384	**Step 3 (bad moduli are rare; hypD item 1).**
   385	- If t is not good, then for some x ∈ 𝒳 and some r′, |E(x; t, r′)| > Ψ_t(x)/(2φ(t)) ≥ Ψ(x)/(4t), by (T).
   386	- So, by Lemma 8.5′ applied at each x ∈ 𝒳 and each of the O(1) dyadic Q covering
   387	  [MV/32, 2MV]·L^{o(1)}:
   388	    #{bad t ∈ 𝒯_Q} ≤ Σ_{x∈𝒳} (4Q/Ψ(x))·CΨ(x)δ_𝒯ε_N ≪ (log ω)·ε_N·#𝒯_Q.
   389	- The hypotheses of Lemma 8.5′ hold:
   390	  - q = s + 1/(4k) + o(1) > 1/(2k) + η₁, because k > (d+1)/4;
   391	  - q < ξ/2 ⟺ h > s − (d−2)/(4k), the level-½ condition, with margin η₀.
   392	- The goodness exponent is a constant 1/2. LV's Definition 5.12 exponent 1 + o(1) is not used, so the
   393	  N^{o(1)} loss that the blind round flagged does not arise.
   394	
   395	**Step 4 (assembly; hypD items 2 and 5).**
   396	- *Good mass.* By Step 1(ii)–(iii) and (Hi) with z ≤ 64:
   397	    Σ_{n∈I_P∩𝒮_B} |𝒢_P(n)| ≥ (M/(CV log log N))·Ψ(I_P) ≥ M²ρ_r/(Cℓ log log N).
   398	- *Bad mass.* By Step 1(i), the n with ℓn bad carry total weight ≤ (2M/V)·#{bad n ≤ 2MV/ℓ}. Averaged
   399	  over ℓ, Step 3 gives
   400	    π_J^{−1} Σ_ℓ #{bad n for ℓ} ≤ (log ω)ε_N·π_J^{−1} Σ_ℓ #{n ≤ 2MV/ℓ : n ∈ 𝒮_B} ≪ (log ω)ε_N MVρ_r/ℓ.
   401	  This is o(1/log log N) times the good mass, uniformly in N.
   402	- *Total.* Summing over P ∈ 𝒫_ℓ, with #𝒫_ℓ ≍ V²/log V and |𝒢| ≍ M², and inserting Step 2:
   403	    E N ≥ (log)^{−O(1)}·(V²/ℓ)·ρ_rρ_F = S²ρ_rρ_F/(log N)^{O(1)}.
   404	- *Item 5.* Small b cannot occur, since b > V/2. Irregular lattices contain no candidates (Lemma 1.3).
   405	  The finite-interval error of sampling g in a square rather than mod ℓ is inside Step 1, because the
   406	  fibres are counted exactly. ∎
   407	
   408	*Remarks.*
   409	- **What was needed beyond hypD §4.1′.**
   410	  - Restricting b to primes in (V/2, V]. This makes every moving residue reduced and removes small b.
   411	  - The 2D field. This makes the rational side a plain interval count, (Hi), with no BV.
   412	  - A residue-free grid 𝒳 for goodness.
   413	- **The 1D design.** The same proof works for (a′_k), with Step 1 replaced by Harper's Theorem 1 for
   414	  the dense moduli family {b}. That needs √ℓS ≤ (bM/ℓ)^{1/2−η}, i.e. k ≥ (3/4)(d+1)(1 + O(η)). We do
   415	  not write it out, since (a″_k) is the design used below.
   416	- **Precision.** The loss is (log N)^{O(1)}, not merely L^{o(1)}. The losses are 1/log V from prime b,
   417	  log log factors from φ(ℓn)/ℓn, and the (log)^{O(1)} of (C8.16).
   418	  - At Horn 1 (k ≍ (log N)^{1/4}), the number of grid points, ε_N and Lemma 8.5′ change. That regime
   419	    still needs the explicit-error rewrite hypD describes.
   420	
   521	
   522	*Remarks.*
   523	1. **Why it works in 2D and not in 1D.** In (a′_k), the rational pair (s, s′) of a pair moves on the
   524	   *line* b′s − bs′ = Δ/ℓ as m varies (hypD §4.2).
   525	   - Divisor switching then sends the problem to (e, e′) solutions of a single linear equation in a
   526	     box. That count is a Diophantine 0/1 quantity with no area main term, and the method gives
   527	     nothing.
   528	   - Averaging over (b, b′, h) does not repair this: the averaged weight is concentrated on pairs with
   529	     good rational approximations b′/b to s′/s.
   530	   - With (g₀, g₁) random, the pair fills a 2D lattice region of area ≫ perimeter·(lattice scale),
   531	     which is all the argument needs. **This is the "key simplification" the prompt asked about. It is
   532	     not a sieve bound: sieve and Rankin bounds fail at density L^{−c}, see below. It is divisor
   533	     switching, made possible by the 2D randomization.**
   534	2. **Sieve and Rankin alternatives (prompt item 3, checked).**
   535	   - Shiu and Nair–Tenenbaum upper bounds for Σ F(n)F(n + h) give only ∏_{B<p≤X}(1 − 2/p) ≍
   536	     (log B/log X)², a logarithmic saving against the needed ρ².
   537	   - A Rankin tilt n^{α−1} that recovers the Dickman exponent cannot be localized to an interval or a
   538	     line: extended to all n it is dominated by small n, and a class mod q receives mass ≥ 1 > L(χ₀)/q
   539	     once q > e^{Cu}.
   540	   - Matomäki–Radziwiłł–Tao-type averaged correlations have o(1) or log-power errors, useless at
   541	     density L^{−c}.
   542	   - Pascadi's Cor. 7.2 gives exponent 13/8 − ε < 2. Codex notes that its coefficient condition can
   543	     hold for large fixed k, but the exponent still falls short.
   544	   - So, as far as we can tell, no sieve-type upper bound with loss L^{o(1)} is available in 1D; codex
   545	     reached the same conclusion.
   546	3. **Precision.** The loss is polylogarithmic, so Theorem R also meets Horn 1's precision requirement
   547	   exp(−o(Λ/k)) for k ≍ (log N)^{1/4}. The remaining Horn-1 obstacles are FM's explicit errors and LD.
   548	
   549	## 4. PC_alg: the kernel transfer, the remaining hypothesis, and why known tools fall short
   550	
   551	### 4.1 Lemma Kern (the 2D kernel lemma) [proved; tested]
   552	
   553	**Setup.** Let d ≥ 3, and let P ≠ P′ be primitive with |P|, |P′| ∈ [V/2, √2V] and Δ = det(P, P′) ≠ 0.
   554	Put v = v(P), v′ = v(P′), and Φ: Z^d → Z², c ↦ (⟨c, v⟩, ⟨c, v′⟩). Write P′ ≡ κP (mod Δ); κ exists
   555	because P is primitive.
   556	
   557	**Statement.**
   558	- (i) Γ := Φ(Z^d) has index |Δ| and equals {(X, X′) : X′ ≡ κ^{d−1}X (mod Δ)}.
   559	- (ii) ker Φ = Q_{PP′}·Z[x, y]_{d−3}, where Q_{PP′} = (bx − ay)(b′x − a′y). This kernel is saturated, and
   560	  its monomial basis has ‖·‖_∞ ≤ 2V² on 𝒫_ℓ, where |a|, |b| ≤ V. In general the bound is
   561	  ≤ 4V² for |P| ≤ √2V, and then H ≥ 2V² is required; only constants change.
   562	- (iii) If H ≥ V², then for every z ∈ Z²,
   563	    Pr_{c∈C_H}[Φ(c) = z] ≤ C_d·|Δ|/(H²‖v ∧ v′‖) ≤ C′_d/(H²V^{2d−4}).
   564	
   565	*Proof.*
   566	- **(ii).** ⟨c, v(P)⟩ = C(P) for the form C = Σ c_i x^{d−1−i}y^i. C(P) = 0 with P primitive gives
   567	  (bx − ay) | C over Z, by Gauss's lemma. Then C(P′) = 0 and det(P, P′) ≠ 0 give (b′x − a′y) | C/(bx − ay).
   568	  So ker Φ = Q·Z[x, y]_{d−3}. A multiple of Q that is integral is an integral multiple, again by Gauss's
   569	  lemma, so the kernel is saturated.
   570	- **(i).** The index of Φ(Z^d) is the gcd of the 2×2 minors of [v; v′], which is |Δ| (hypD Prop G(i),
   571	  (I6); also codex pcest_1, via a unimodular change sending P to (1, 0)). Since P′ ≡ κP (mod Δ),
   572	  v′ ≡ κ^{d−1}v (mod Δ), so Γ lies in the displayed lattice, which also has index |Δ|. Hence they are
   573	  equal.
   574	- **(iii).**
   575	  - A fibre is (c₀ + K) ∩ C_H, with K = ker Φ of rank d − 2 and det K = ‖v ∧ v′‖/|Δ|. Here we use that
   576	    a saturated lattice and its orthogonal complement in Z^d have the same determinant, and that the row
   577	    lattice of [v; v′] has index |Δ| in its saturation.
   578	  - The translates c + F, where F is the fundamental cell of the basis in (ii), are disjoint. They lie
   579	    in the section of the cube [−(2d−3)H, (2d−3)H]^d by a (d−2)-flat, whose volume is ≤ C_dH^{d−2}.
   580	  - So the fibre has ≤ C_dH^{d−2}|Δ|/‖v ∧ v′‖ points. Divide by (2H + 1)^d.
   581	  - For the second inequality: every 2×2 minor of [v; v′] equals Δ·W_{ij}(P, P′), with W bihomogeneous
   582	    of bidegree (d−2, d−2). For example W_{01} = (aa′)^{d−2}.
   583	  - The vector W(P, P′) of all divided minors never vanishes on (S¹)² (repaired after codex pcest_2).
   584	    - Off the diagonal and antidiagonal, this is injectivity of the projective Veronese map: v(P) ∦ v(P′)
   585	      when P ∦ P′, and Δ ≠ 0.
   586	    - On the diagonal, W(P, P) is the wedge of v(P) with its tangent ∂_u v(P) divided by det(P, u). It
   587	      is nonzero because the rational normal curve is nondegenerate. For example, at P = (1, 0) the
   588	      adjacent divided minor W_{01} equals 1. The antidiagonal is the same up to sign.
   589	    - W is continuous, so by compactness ‖v ∧ v′‖ ≥ c_d|Δ|(|P||P′|)^{d−2}. ∎
   590	    - **Effective version for candidates (Opus proof critic).** The minor (d−2, d−1) equals
   591	      (bb′)^{d−2}Δ. On 𝒫_ℓ we have b, b′ > V/2, so ‖v ∧ v′‖ ≥ |Δ|(V/2)^{2d−4} directly, without any
   592	      compactness argument. This is all that Prop 4.2 uses.
   593	    - The exact identity det K·|Δ| = ‖v ∧ v′‖ and the bound with c_d > 0.05 (d ≤ 7) are checked exactly
   594	      in `tests/test_pcest.py`.
   595	
   596	*Cost [proved arithmetic].*
   597	- H ≥ V² = ℓS² means h > 1/(2k), which raises the h-floor from LV's 1/(4k) (FM) to 1/(2k).
   598	- Then θ = 2s + (d−1)/(4k) + max(1/(2k), s − (d−2)/(4k)). The floor binds only for k < d(d+1)/4,
   599	  against (d² − 1)/4 before.
   600	- **The infimum over k is unchanged.**
   601	- Finite-k examples: d = 9, k = 20 gives θ = 0.325 instead of 0.3125, so c = 0.937 instead of 0.928. At
   602	  d = 9 the minimal k with c < 1 moves from 11 to 12 (`src/pcest/params.py`). Codex's (5) agrees.
   603	- If one also wanted the moduli G|Δ to stay below |F|^{1/2} (not needed below), the condition would
   604	  become h > s + (6 − d)/(4k) (codex).
   605	
   606	### 4.2 Reduction of PC to one arithmetic hypothesis
   607	
   608	**Notation.** For a pair (P, P′) ∈ 𝒫_ℓ² with ℓ | Δ, and g ∈ 𝒢 ∩ L_ℓ(P):
   609	- G = G_g(P), G′ = G_g(P′), and T := dHV^{d−1};
   610	- the full windows I := F̂_g(P) + G·[−T, T] and I′ likewise;
   611	- the lift classes r_j := F̂_g(P) + Gj (mod G|Δ|) and r′_j := F̂_g(P′) + G′κ^{d−1}j (mod G′|Δ|), for
   612	  j mod |Δ|.
   613	
   614	**The normalized lift correlation.**
   615	  κ_alg(ℓ, P, P′, g) := |Δ|·Σ_{j mod |Δ|} Ψ_I(G|Δ|, r_j)·Ψ_{I′}(G′|Δ|, r′_j) / (4T²·ϱ(T|G|, B)·ϱ(T|G′|, B)),
   616	where Ψ_I(m, r) := #{F ∈ I ∩ 𝒮_B : F ≡ r (mod m)}. If the smooth numbers of each window were spread
   617	evenly over the |Δ| lift classes, κ_alg would be ≍ 1.
   618	
   619	**Prop 4.2 [proved].** Assume h ≥ 1/(2k) + η₀. For each such (ℓ, P, P′, g),
   620	  Pr_c[F_A(P), F_A(P′) ∈ 𝒮_B] ≤ C_d·(V²/|Δ|)·ϱ(T|G|)ϱ(T|G′|)·κ_alg.
   621	*Proof.*
   622	- By Lemma Kern(iii), Pr_c ≤ C′_d(H²V^{2d−4})^{−1}·#{z ∈ Γ ∩ [−T, T]² : F̂ + Gz₁ and F̂′ + G′z₂ smooth}.
   623	- By Lemma Kern(i), that count is Σ_j Ψ_I(G|Δ|, r_j)Ψ_{I′}(G′|Δ|, r′_j).
   624	- Insert the definition of κ_alg and T² = d²H²V^{2d−2}. ∎
   625	
   626	Here V²/|Δ| = S²/q is comparable to (not equal to) the bounding-box factor 1/sin∠(P, P′).
   627	- If κ_alg were ≤ L^{o(1)}, the factor would cost only O(log S) on average. For fixed P and q, the
   628	  equation det(P, P′) = ±ℓq has O(1) solutions P′ in the box (they lie on two lines parallel to P,
   629	  spaced by |P| ≥ V/2).
   630	- So Σ over pairs of (S²/q)·(Theorem R's uniform per-pair bound) loses only a harmonic sum
   631	  Σ_{q≤2S²} 1/q.
   632	- LD keeps the product (S²/q)·κ_alg; the two factors are not separated.
   633	
   634	**Hypothesis LD (lift dispersion under rational-smoothness weights) [open].** Uniformly in N:
   635	  E_ℓ Σ_{P≠P′, ℓ|Δ} |𝒢|^{−1} Σ_{g∈𝒢∩L_ℓ(P)} Y_PY_{P′}·(S²/q)·κ_alg ≤ L^{o(1)}·E_ℓ Σ_{P≠P′, ℓ|Δ} |𝒢|^{−1} Σ_g Y_PY_{P′}.
   636	- It is an upper bound, at precision L^{o(1)}, for a restricted second moment of smooth numbers in the
   637	  lift classes of moving residues to moduli G|Δ| = ℓ²nq.
   638	- The weights are the actual rational-smooth multiplicities. Theorem R shows that the right side is
   639	  ≤ (log N)^{O(1)}S⁴ρ_r².
   640	- LD is implied by a suitably weighted form of hypD's RBDH′ (an o(1) relative variance), but it is
   641	  *weaker*: it is an upper bound within L^{o(1)}. It is codex pcest_1's (6) with the kernel transfer
   642	  carried out. We do not claim an equivalence.
   643	
   644	**Theorem 4.3 [proved implication; repaired after codex pcest_2].**
   645	- **Hypotheses.** Those of Theorem FM, with h ≥ 1/(2k) + η₀ and S² := (log N)^{C₃}/(ρ_rρ_F), where C₃ is
   646	  larger than the polylog exponent lost in Theorem FM. Let N_ℓ(A) be the **filtered** count of §1
   647	  (|G(P)| ≥ MV/32).
   648	- **Conclusions.**
   649	  - (i) FM + Theorem R + Prop 4.2 + LD ⟹ PC := E[N(N−1)]/(E N)² ≤ L^{o(1)}.
   650	  - (ii) Hence, by Prop PZ (E N ≥ 1), D_k(γ) holds with γ ≥ L^{−o(1)}.
   651	*Proof.*
   652	- E[N(N−1)] ≤ E_ℓ Σ_{P≠P′, ℓ|Δ} |𝒢|^{−1} Σ_{g: |G|,|G′| ≥ MV/32} Y_PY_{P′} Pr_c[·].
   653	- By the filter, T|G| ∈ [TMV/32, 2TMV], so ϱ(T|G|) ≤ 64ϱ(TMV) ≤ ρ_F(log N)^{O(1)} by (Hi) and (C8.16).
   654	  - The unfiltered version of this step was wrong: for |G| ≈ ℓ, the ratio X_F/(T|G|) is a positive
   655	    power of N.
   656	- By positivity, LD (stated over all g) bounds the filtered sum.
   657	- So, by Prop 4.2, LD and Theorem R, E[N(N−1)] ≤ C_dρ_F²·L^{o(1)}·S⁴ρ_r²(log N)^{O(1)}.
   658	- By Theorem FM, which only counts |G| ∈ [MV/32, 2MV] and so applies to the filtered count,
   659	  (E N)² ≥ S⁴ρ_r²ρ_F²/(log)^{O(1)} and E N ≥ 1.
   660	- Theorem R's condition S² ≥ ρ_r^{−2} holds for large N, since ρ_F/ρ_r = L^{−c+o(1)} for some c > 0. ∎
   661	- (The first version took S² = L^ε/(ρ_rρ_F), giving L^{O(ε)} losses that are not L^{o(1)}. The
   662	  polylogarithmic choice of S² fixes this.)
   663	
   664	### 4.3 Why LD is not available, and what each known tool gives
   665	
   666	All items below are statements about what the cited tools give by themselves. They are not lower bounds
   667	on LD, and they do not exclude proofs that combine these tools with further arithmetic information.
   668	
   669	- **(B1′) Naive use of Harper's BDH (all residues, arbitrary residue) loses the factor G.** For one
   670	  modulus m = G|Δ|:
   671	  - LD needs the |Δ| lift classes, with main-term mass |Δ|(Ψ/m)² = Ψ²/(G·m).
   672	  - Harper's Theorem 2 (Q ≤ Ψ) controls Σ_{m≤Q} Σ_{all reduced r}|E(m, r)|², a global sum whose
   673	    per-modulus scale is Ψ²/m.
   674	  - Harper's asymptotic BDH for general sequences (J. London Math. Soc. 112 (2025) e70293,
   675	    arXiv 2412.19644, Cor. 1) confirms that the all-residue variance is dominated by its diagonal QΨ.
   676	    It has no restricted-moduli or restricted-residue form either.
   677	  - Positivity cannot recover the factor G = N^{s+1/(4k)+o(1)}, and e^{−cu/log²u} cannot compensate a
   678	    polynomial factor (codex pcest_1, Q2). The modulus family is also sparse, with density
   679	    ≈ ρ_r/(ℓ log Y), so restricted versions are needed anyway, as in barrier B1.
   680	- **(B2′) Divisor switching needs small moduli.** Lemma DS works because q ≤ y^{O(1)}.
   681	  - Applied to F in a progression mod G ≈ N^{s+1/(4k)}, the complementary-factor count is only
   682	    nontrivial when the divisor cutoff is D ≲ X_F/G.
   683	  - The divisor sum is then evaluated at density ϱ(X_F/(Gy)) instead of ϱ(X_F), a loss of L^{Θ(1)}.
   684	  - The q-part of the coupling (modulus q ≤ 2S²) is small. But it sits inside an AP to the large
   685	    modulus G, so the same obstruction applies.
