   745	
   746	**Assumption (LV6) [open; inherited from lemmaM Corollary DD and hypD Corollary K].** LV §6
   747	(characters, square roots, the residual square-class kernel) has to be adapted to:
   748	- pure-descent rows from pairs of height ≤ V = N^{1/(4k)}L^{O(1)};
   749	- the non-monic pair (f, g₁x − g₀);
   750	- the one extra g₁-parity column.
   751	The adaptation must keep the cost (rows)^{2+o(1)} and the transcript invariance (T) of Theorem DD.
   752	LV's Theorem 2.6 is written for heights L(1/3). The adaptation has not been written out, by lemmaM,
   753	hypD or us.
   754	
   755	**Theorem A [conditional on LD and LV6].**
   756	- A possibly cheaper alternative to A′, not checked: Bernstein's deterministic batch smoothness
   757	  algorithm, run over batches of ≥ B/polylog candidates gathered across R-trials and fields, might
   758	  remove HECM at no cost to the exponent (Opus proof critic).
   759	- **Statement.**
   760	  - Fix odd d ≥ 3, k > (d+1)/4, η₀ > 0, and let θ := θ(d, k) = 2s + (d−1)/(4k) + max(1/(2k),
   761	    s − (d−2)/(4k)) with θ < 1.
   762	  - Let h be the corresponding height plus η₀, let a := θ/√2, and B = L[1/2, a].
   763	  - Assume LD for design (a″_k) at these parameters, uniformly in N, and LV6.
   764	  - Then every N is factored in expected time L_N[1/2, (1+θ)/√2 + O(η₀) + o(1)].
   765	- **Consequences.** Infimum over k: (1 + 3/(d+1))/√2, i.e. 0.9723 (d = 7), 0.9192 (d = 9), 0.8839
   766	  (d = 11) and 0.8132 (d = 19). The constant is < 1 iff d ≥ 7, with k ≥ 13 (d = 7) or k ≥ 12 (d = 9).
   767	- **Further explicit assumptions (codex pcest_2).**
   768	  - (HECM) LPP's running-time theorem for the hyperelliptic curve method, as used by LV Cor. 3.19. The
   769	    published proof trail is incomplete (LPP II defers details to an unpublished Part III); Theorem A′
   770	    removes this assumption.
   771	  - Reducible f are rejected. The irreducibility transfer omits the O(log N) primes dividing g₁N; the
   772	    exceptional probability is polynomially small.
   773	  - The g₁ column records the parity of the number of atomic special-q relations in an assembled row,
   774	    counted with multiplicity.
   775	- **Ingredients and their status.**
   776	  - Proved: Lemma GB, Theorem FM, Theorem R, Lemma Kern, Prop 4.2, Theorem 4.3.
   777	  - From hypD and lemmaM: Theorems DD, S′, K and Corollary K's algorithm.
   778	  - Prior art: Lenstra–Pomerance as the shape test, and HECM for the B-smoothness tests (caveat below).
   779	
   780	*Proof.*
   781	1. Preprocess by trial division to B.
   782	2. Theorem 4.3 gives D_k(γ) with γ ≥ L^{−o(1)}.
   783	3. hypD Theorem K(i)–(iii) and its field-selection rule give pure-descent rows at expected cost
   784	   (T_shape + T_SQ)·L^{o(1)} each, with T_shape = L_N[1/2, 1/√2] (LP on √N-size integers) and
   785	   T_SQ = S²L^{o(1)} = L^{θ/(2a)+o(1)}.
   786	4. B·L^{o(1)} rows, LV6 and Theorem DD give a nontrivial factor with probability ≥ 1/2 per dependency.
   787	5. Cost: L[1/2, a + max(1/√2, θ/(2a))] + L[1/2, 2a]. At a = θ/√2 this is L[1/2, (1+θ)/√2], since
   788	   θ ≤ 1. ∎
   789	
   790	**Theorem A′ (no HECM) [conditional on LD and LV6].**
   791	- Replace HECM by the deterministic Pollard–Strassen method, which finds all prime factors ≤ B of m in
   792	  B^{1/2+o(1)}(log m)^{O(1)} time (Pollard, Proc. Camb. Phil. Soc. 76 (1974); Strassen, Jber. DMV 78
   793	  (1976); Costa–Harvey, Math. Comp. 83 (2014)).
   794	- Each smoothness test then costs L^{a/2}, and
   795	    c_PS = min_a max(2a, a + max(1/√2, θ/(2a) + a/2)).
   796	- For θ ≤ 1/2 the optimum is at a² − √2a + θ = 0, giving c_PS = 1/√2 + (√2 − √(2 − 4θ))/2.
   797	- θ → 3/(d+1) gives d = 7: 1.0607; d = 9: 0.9670 (a = 0.2599); d = 11: 0.9142; d = 19: 0.8226. These
   798	  were corrected after codex pcest_2; the first version printed 0.9671 and 0.9144 from a grid search.
   799	- So **the HECM caveat (LPP Part III unpublished) can be removed at a cost, and c < 1 survives for
   800	  d ≥ 9**.
   801	- A possibly cheaper route, not checked: Pomerance's (1987) rigorous ECM detection of *most* smooth
   802	  numbers. Whether a "most smooth values" guarantee suffices for FM and PZ (literature critic) is open. LV6 might itself use HECM; if so, the same replacement applies there at L^{o(1)}-many tests
   803	  per row. We did not check LV §6 for this.
   804	
   805	**What exactly is missing for a theorem beating Lenstra–Pomerance [the sharpest form we can state].**
   806	1. **LD** (§4.2): a restricted, rational-smoothness-weighted BDH upper bound at precision L^{o(1)} for
   807	   smooth numbers in the |Δ| lift classes of a reduced residue to moduli ℓ²nq. Here n is B-smooth,
   808	   q ≤ 2S², the moduli are ≍ N^{s+3/(4k)}L^{O(1)}, and the values are ≍ N^{s+h+d/(4k)}.
   809	2. **LV6** (bookkeeping of a known method at new heights; not expected to need a new idea, but not
   810	   written).
   811	
   812	Within this project's proved results, nothing else is missing. FM, the binary rational correlation,
   813	the kernel transfer, the split counting (Theorem S′), the complete-target-list reduction (Theorem K)
   814	and fruitfulness (DD) are proved here or in hypD/lemmaM.
   815	
   816	Caveats:
   817	- these are internal, unrefereed results;
   818	- the HECM running-time theorem (Theorem A only; A′ avoids it);
   819	- the Lemma 5.1 irreducibility transfer, checked at the level of statements.
   820	
   821	**Has anyone beaten c = 1 rigorously (1992–2026)?** To our knowledge, no. Codex pcest_1 and pcest_2 and
   822	the Opus literature critic (§7) all searched. Neither found a rigorous algorithm factoring every N in L[1/2, c] with c < 1. The
   823	rigorous record remains Lenstra–Pomerance 1992 (JAMS 5, 483–516).
   824	- Related results are either heuristic (NFS; Stange 2022) or concern other problems:
   825	  - Kleinjung–Wesolowski: discrete logarithms in finite fields of fixed characteristic (JAMS 35, 2022).
   826	  - de Boer–Pellet-Mary–Wesolowski (arXiv 2512.01588): ERH, class and unit groups.
   827	- Under GRH the best rigorous constant is also c = 1: Seysen and A. K. Lenstra's class-group method,
   828	  whose GRH dependence Lenstra–Pomerance removed. We know of no c < 1 under a standard hypothesis.
   829	- Lee–Venkatesan (J. Number Theory 187 (2018) 92–159) produce congruences in L[1/3], but their
   830	  factoring conclusion is
   831	  conditional on their Conjecture 7.1.
   832	- This is a search result, not a certification.
