88:## 1. Q1: the Farey premise needs an admissibility correction
113:Thus **FALSE for the actual admissible fractions** is the assertion that arbitrarily large K-fold Farey multiplicities remain in this filtered, prime-b design. The argument also explains why removing the filter or the prime-b/reduced-fraction condition changes the issue.
122:For odd prime powers the unit group is cyclic and the kernel has size at most d; for powers of 2 its cyclic factors give at most 2d. CRT proves (1.2). A pointwise assertion J_d(t)=L^{o(1)} for all polynomial-size smooth t is not made.
133:Proof: use Rankin with alpha_R in (SP). The weighted Euler product divided by zeta(alpha_R,B) is
150:The weighted version of Theorem AR below, proved at the end of §3, makes this replacement valid even when lift energy is attached to n. Therefore the **unweighted marginal sampling** of the original structured residues causes no L-scale loss. This does not include the partner's rational-smoothness indicator; that distinction is essential.
152:**PROVED model barrier without primitivity.** Take t prime, MV=c t with a fixed 0<c<1, and let K->infinity with K log V=o(V) and V<=M. Allow all numerators and denominators in [V/2,V] times [M/2,M]. Choose primitive (b_0,g_0) in [0.55V/K,0.60V/K] times [0.55M/K,0.60M/K]. Mobius inversion gives Theta(MV/K^2) choices: its error is O((M/K)log(V/K)), smaller than the area under the condition on K. Each has Theta(K) scalar multiples in the original box. The determinant of two of the smaller vectors has magnitude <t, so distinct primitive vectors give distinct modular slopes. Moreover the determinant of a smaller vector and any vector in the original box has magnitude O(MV/K)<t. Thus another vector in that box with its modular slope must be a scalar multiple; the representation count is Theta(K), not merely bounded below by K. The counting measure of high-multiplicity residues is Theta(K^{-2}), while their representation-weighted measure is Theta(K^{-1}). An energy of size K^2 on them has bounded all-residue mean and representation-weighted mean Theta(K). Taking K=L^epsilon with epsilon>0 small makes this an impermissible loss and still satisfies the geometric size conditions. This is a countermodel to an inference from box area and all-residue L^2 alone. It is not a counterexample to smooth-number dispersion and it does not satisfy (1.1).
180:**PROVED location of the loss.** After a marginal all-residue bound one can discard 1_B(n') by positivity. This replaces its expected density rho_r by 1 and loses
183: \rho_r^{-1}=L^{(s-\nu)/(2a)+o(1)}.
187:Alternatively, keeping the partner density by a fibre divisor-switching bound makes one take the supremum of the attached lift energy over that fibre. Controlling the unweighted average of that energy does not bound this supremum or its correlation with 1_B(n'). In the max-coarse-class version this can cost up to the coarse modulus t; it cannot be called L^{o(1)}.
189:**PROVED information-theoretic countermodel.** On each fibre let a binary partner weight have density p=rho_r. Give its support lift energy 1/p and give the complement energy 1. The unweighted mean energy is below 2; the weighted mean is 1, whereas the desired independent scale is p. The loss is 1/p. This can be repeated at every outer index ell,q,n. Even exact uniformity of coarse-class totals is compatible with the model: inside the selected coarse classes put their fixed total mass on only a p fraction of the fine lifts. Since rho_F<p and |Delta| is polynomial, the model respects the per-lift capacity after rounding. It is a model of what marginal estimates cannot imply, not an arithmetic disproof of LD*.
191:**CONJECTURED for (a''_k).** The actual mixed correlation in (2.1), with the smooth-number lift energies, satisfies LD*. Neither this model nor the Farey calculation settles that assertion. In particular, outer averaging is not by itself a proof of independence.
465:**PROVED weighted extension.** (AR) and (3.12) remain valid with a factor A^{omega(n)}, fixed A, inside their sums. Here are the extra checks, to avoid multiplying unrelated averages. Replace the Rankin product in (SP) for the n count by the product in (1.3), at cost E_A=exp(O_A(u_R+log log B))(log N)^C=L^{o(1)}. When h|n, submultiplicativity gives A^{omega(n)}<=A^{omega(h)}A^{omega(n/h)}, so (3.7) gains E_A A^{omega(h)}. The latter is <=C_epsilon h^epsilon for every fixed epsilon>0. With epsilon=0.01 and tau(r)<=r^{0.01}, the exceptional dyadic exponent is still < -0.05. In the large-conductor terms the maximum weight is N^{epsilon_0} with epsilon_0 chosen smaller, and is absorbed by the same polynomial gaps. In §3.4 the small-layer u sum gains A^{omega(u)} and still converges, since its exponent is <-1.9; the large-layer weight can again be absorbed in a polynomial saving. Small and generic conductor contributions gain E_A. This proves the extension with a uniform L^{o(1)} bound.
467:Together with §1, this proves the unweighted marginal structured-residue assertion at the relevant averaged normalization: if K_{t,D}(r)=Dt^2 Psi(x)^{-2}sum_j A_{tD}(r+tj;x)^2, then
